A||||8||Lisa 86 2m4||Liquid Engine overhaul kits||||5||
Z||000000||Lisa 86 2m4||12-23-2000||10:35 AM||||Hi everybody! Such knowledge and Fiero wisdom all in one place! I love this board! Anyway, I've had my fiero for 9 years now and this month I plan on doing alot of work on it, so you may see more posts from me....About a year and a half ago, I had the engine rebuilt, recently there has been blue smoke coming from the exhaust noticeable when I've been idleing for a few minutes then accelerate.  I've been getting blowby in the air filter and the pcv hose. I ordered from JC Whitney this pellet and liquid engine kit, and now I am leary.  Has anyone out there tried this? Is it safe for my car, or should I send it back? All help is appreciated! I am also installing a keyless entry system, outer dew wipes, refinishing my rims, buffing the scratches out of the glass and am thinking about putting in those driving lights.  If anyone would like to give me tips or how to's on any of those things, that would be great!! Thanks everybody!<BR>Lisa||||reg||5
Z||000001||80's BOY||12-23-2000||11:02 AM||||hi lisa, and welcome to the forum. i don't have any info on that subject, but i get the feeling you'll get plenty of responses throughout the day. glad to have you aboard!||||reg||14
Z||000002||theogre||12-23-2000||12:21 PM||||JC Whitless and other places have been selling that stuff for decades. If it does anything it will be temporary. <P>It sounds like whoever did your rebuild did a bloody poor job. The problem you are describing sounds allot like it needs valve work. <P>The question becomes did they rebuild the whole motor or just the bottom half. If they only did the bottom now the top is failing. If they did the whole thing then it was a really cruddy job.||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||12-23-2000||02:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Lisa 86 2m4:<BR><B>...blue smoke coming from the exhaust noticeable when I've been idling for a few minutes then accelerate...getting blowby in the air filter and the pcv hose...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>...doesn't blowby normally point to piston ring problems?  Educate me, please  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||5
Z||000004||mbramble||12-23-2000||03:36 PM||||Back in '74, as a young sailor stationed at Great Lakes Naval base, I had a '66 Plymouth Valiant that burned oil badly.  I drove down into Chicago and bought a package of the "pellets and liquid" from the J.C. Whitney store.  Put the pellets in the cylinders and the liquid in the tank and it did absolutely nothing at all.  Nothing.  It was a waste of $9.95 but at least I got to visit that famous store!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||strdream||12-23-2000||05:25 PM||||Hi Lisa<BR>I question the credibility of anyone that says they can fix anything with the contents of a can.<BR>  Stay with me on this.  <BR> That being said, I knew a guy that tried the stuff and could not say enough GOOD about it. He was sold on it.<BR>  My feeling are,  What can it heart!  If the engine is worn out you sure aren't going to harm it any by adding some goo to it. <BR>  I think before I did anything I would do some more diagnosis.<BR>   How is the overall performance?<BR>   how much oil does it go thru<BR>   when it was "rebiult" did it get bored out and new pistons?<BR>   A bad PCV can cause blow by to go into the air filter. All engines have blowby and that is what the PCV is for.  The PCV should be able to handle all of the blowby.  Check the vac. to the PCV as well<BR>   If you can conferm the PCV as a problem than I'm with Oger on the valve seals.<BR>  If you have a good PCV system than you have piston and ring trouble also as Stu said.  <BR>Let us know!<BR>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||12-23-2000||06:42 PM||||DONT BOTHER LISA, YOUD DO JUST AS MUCH GOOD IF YOU THREW ASPIRIN IN THE SPARK PLUG HOLES.<P><BR>Those are usually the symtoms of worn valve guides/valve stem wear<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-23-2000).]||||reg||13
Z||000007||FieroJoe||12-23-2000||07:37 PM||||As for the dewstrips, you'll definatly need a special tool, or modify an existing one.  I could post all the directions on here, as I just got done doing both my inner and outer ones, but you'll find that info, and a lot more by either searching through the archives of this forum, or visiting the Fiero Online Service guide  <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/osg.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/osg.html</A> <P>Also, lots of great wisdom can be found in the ogre's cave - the link is on the bottom of every forum page.<P>Hope it all comes out well and merry christmas!<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||12-23-2000||08:22 PM||||I missed the part about there apearing to be blowby. <P>All engine have some blowby. Do check the PCV line and valve. If tbe blowby is within normal limits the PCV should be collecting it.<P>The givaway here is the oil on takeoff. That's usually valves. <P>It's posible for the exhaust valve stems to leak bad enough to cause blowby but most often its a ring problem.<P>Since you already have the stuff, you may as well try it. The engine needs major work as it is. I doubt you'll hurt it anymore than it already is. Lets say the stuff cost $10 to buy ignoring shipping carges you won't get back anyway. by the time you send it back and pay return shipping you'll get at most 6-7 dollars. ||||reg||1
